"global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2026, "rank": 159, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2026, "rank": 55, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2026, "rank": 160, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2025, "rank": 158, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2025, "rank": 56, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2025, "rank": 227, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 163, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500--wholesalers", "title": "Fortune Sector Leaders: Wholesalers", "year": 2024, "rank": 1, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 54, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 186, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 56, "itemDescription": "Sysco, a food marketer and distributor, jumped up 14 spots on the Fortune 500 and posted a 34% increase in revenue in 2022, as restaurant demand\u2014abnormally low during the height of the pandemic\u2014rebounded.<br />\r\n<ul><br />\r\n \t<li>The majority of Sysco's sales come from operations in the U.S., but international growth outstripped domestic in 2022, partly due to the reversal of more stringent pandemic restrictions abroad.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>The company sued a slew of meat companies in 2022, alleging that they violated antitrust regulations through price fixing. Although Sysco moved to settle the lawsuits, it is now mired in a legal battle with Burford Capital, which financed Sysco\u2019s litigation.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>The workforce for the food marketer and distributor grew 22% in size from 2021, the third largest increase among the top 100 companies in the Fortune 500.</li><br />\r\n</ul>"}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 261, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 70, "itemDescription": "<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Food product marketer and distributor Sysco continued its slide down the Fortune 500 this year as the pandemic kept restaurant demand largely depressed. The company dropped 10 spots on this list, its revenue decreasing 3% to $51 billion. However, it managed to more than double its profits year over year, to $524 million\u2014a fact that reflects the gradual loosening of COVID-related restrictions throughout the year. In its fourth quarter, the company\u2019s sales finally surpassed 2019 quarterly volume levels\u2014another encouraging sign.</span>"}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 199, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;The coronavirus chewed through Sysco\u2019s core grub-supplying business as restaurants around the world shuttered last year. Revenues for Sysco, America\u2019s biggest wholesale food distributor, fell 12% year on year to $52.9 billion, a decline that caused it to slide four places in the Fortune 500 rankings. COVID-19 took a bite out of profits, too, causing a stomach-churning 87% drop to $215 million. The Houston-based company pivoted amid pandemic-related closures to help its eatery customers offer alternative options, like takeout-meal kits and contactless menus. But Sysco\u2019s financial prospects are mostly tied to people\u2019s desire to dine out again as the economy reopens.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri, Arial&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\" data-sheets-textstyleruns=\"{&quot;1&quot;:0}\uee10{&quot;1&quot;:280,&quot;2&quot;:{&quot;6&quot;:1}}\uee10{&quot;1&quot;:287}\">The coronavirus chewed through Sysco\u2019s core grub-supplying business as restaurants around the world shuttered last year. Revenues for Sysco, America\u2019s biggest wholesale food distributor, fell 12% year on year to $52.9 billion, a decline that caused it to slide 20 places in the Global 500 rankings. COVID-19 took a bite out of profits, too, causing a stomach-churning 87% drop to $215 million. The Houston, Texas-based company pivoted amid pandemic-related closures to help its eatery customers offer alternative options, like takeout-meal kits and contactless menus. But Sysco\u2019s financial prospects are mostly tied to people\u2019s desire to dine out again as the economy reopens.</span>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 60, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;The coronavirus chewed through Sysco\u2019s core grub-supplying business as restaurants around the world shuttered last year. Revenues for Sysco, America\u2019s biggest wholesale food distributor, fell 12% year on year to $52.9 billion, a decline that caused it to slide four places in the Fortune 500 rankings. COVID-19 took a bite out of profits, too, causing a stomach-churning 87% drop to $215 million. The Houston-based company pivoted amid pandemic-related closures to help its eatery customers offer alternative options, like takeout-meal kits and contactless menus. But Sysco\u2019s financial prospects are mostly tied to people\u2019s desire to dine out again as the economy reopens.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri, Arial&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\" data-sheets-textstyleruns=\"{&quot;1&quot;:0}\uee10{&quot;1&quot;:280,&quot;2&quot;:{&quot;6&quot;:1}}\uee10{&quot;1&quot;:287}\">The coronavirus chewed through Sysco\u2019s core grub-supplying business as restaurants around the world shuttered last year. Revenues for Sysco, America\u2019s biggest wholesale food distributor, fell 12% year on year to $52.9 billion, a decline that caused it to slide four places in the Fortune 500 rankings. COVID-19 took a bite out of profits, too, causing a stomach-churning 87% drop to $215 million. The Houston-based company pivoted amid pandemic-related closures to help its eatery customers offer alternative options, like takeout-meal kits and contactless menus. But Sysco\u2019s financial prospects are mostly tied to people\u2019s desire to dine out again as the economy reopens.</span>"}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 179, "itemDescription": "Sysco is America\u2019s biggest and most profitable wholesale food distributor. While the Houston company took in $60.1 billion in 2019, up 2.4% from the year prior, it slid seven places in the Global 500 rankings. Rising food costs, due to inflation and international tariffs, plus higher labor expenses, exacerbated by a shortage of commercial truck drivers, gnawed at the bottom line. Regardless, Sysco grew profits by 17% to $1.6 billion, partly through layoffs, subsidiary consolidation in France, and facility closures in Canada and Europe. Now, as the coronavirus pandemic shutters restaurants around the world, Sysco\u2019s new CEO, Kevin Hourican, formerly of CVS Health, will have to figure out how to keep the business cooking."}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 56, "itemDescription": "Sysco is America\u2019s biggest and most profitable wholesale food distributor. While the Houston company took in $60.1 billion in 2019, up 2.4% from the year prior, it slid two places in the <em>Fortune</em> 500 rankings. Rising food costs, due to inflation and international tariffs, plus higher labor expenses, exacerbated by a shortage of commercial truck drivers, gnawed at the bottom line. Regardless, Sysco grew profits by 17% to $1.6 billion, partly through layoffs, subsidiary consolidation in France, and facility closures in Canada and Europe. Now, as the coronavirus pandemic shutters restaurants around the world, Sysco\u2019s new CEO, Kevin Hourican, formerly of CVS Health, will have to figure out how to keep the business cooking."}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 172, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 54, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 174, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 54, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2017, "rank": 179, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2017, "rank": 57, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2016, "rank": 188, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2016, "rank": 57, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2015, "rank": 229, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2015, "rank": 61, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2014, "rank": 234, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2014, "rank": 63, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2013, "rank": 247, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2013, "rank": 65, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2012, "rank": 262, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2012, "rank": 69, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortun